<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <!--
      On so the compiler checks doc comments, and so CS1591 - "missing doc comment on a public
      member" - is an error everywhere. That was once suppressed here while a comment pass worked
      through the projects one at a time; the pass is finished, the suppression is gone, and every
      public member in the solution carries a comment because the build will not complete otherwise.

      What it checks is malformed XML and a <param> naming an argument that does not exist. This
      used to claim it also catches two <summary> elements on one member, and it does not - Roslyn
      has no diagnostic for a repeated <summary>, so the generated XML simply carries both. Five
      members were quietly doing it, each with a summary stranded above the wrong field from an
      edit that inserted something between them.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1848;CA1031</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Product>Stellaris Empire Manager</Product>
    <Company>Alex Ashmore</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) Alex Ashmore</Copyright>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Kezyma/Stellaris-Empire-Manager</RepositoryUrl>

    <!--
      Without this the SDK appends "+<git SHA>" to the informational version, because SourceLink is
      on by default. Nothing asked for that and one thing read it: the desktop stamps the
      informational version into its extracted game data and rebuilds the cache when it changes. So
      every commit threw away 223MB of artwork and re-rendered nine thousand portraits, for a change
      that may not have touched extraction at all - and rewrote the committed web data as well,
      which made a one-line diff out of every build.

      What extraction produces is described by what extraction is, and that is measured properly
      now: see GameDataExtractor.ExtractorVersion.
    -->
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deterministic builds keep round-trip fixture hashes stable across machines.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)' == ''">
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
